  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 1131 AM MST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nWith yesterday's shortwave moving off to the east and an upper-level \nridge building behind it, conditions over the next couple of days \nwill be fairly quiet weather-wise. Highs will be in the upper \n40s/low 50s along the I-25 corridor and across the plains today and \ntomorrow, with the mountain valleys seeing highs in the upper 30s to \nlow 40s. Thursday will see highs in the mid 50s for the urban \ncorridor and plains, along with clear skies and light winds, making \nfor a rather nice, sunny Thanksgiving. Friday should then cap off \nthe week as our warmest day, as highs in the urban corridor/plains \nare expected to reach into the low 60s. \n\nThe upper-level ridge should also start to weaken on Friday, with \nthe next system building to the north and west of Colorado.\nWest/northwest flow aloft will start to strengthen, allowing for \nstronger winds (20-30mph sustained, 40-50mph gusts) along the \nnorthern Front Range. There's currently some uncertainty within the \nmodels regarding the exact timing of the strongest winds; the NBM \nhas peak wind gusts occurring early Friday morning, between 5am-8am, \nwhile the deterministic GFS leans towards keeping any stronger gusts \nin the afternoon, between 12pm-6pm. Regardless, the strongest winds \nwill occur at the mountain peaks and higher elevations, and with \nrelative humidities expected to stay above 30% across the Front \nRange & foothills, fire weather shouldn't be much of a concern.\n\nThings get more uncertain as we head into the weekend. Some model \nruns, namely the GFS/GEFS, favor a shallower trough, with the jet \nstaying north of Colorado, while the ECMWF favors a deeper trough, \nwith the jet placed over western Colorado. Additionally, the last \nseveral runs of the GFS have an initial weak shortwave move across \nthe state Saturday into Sunday, followed by a deeper trough on \nMonday, while the ECMWF resolves only one system for the Saturday-\nSunday time frame. A lot of this uncertainty comes from \ndifferences in how the models are handling the interactions of the\nupper-level ridge over the western U.S. with both a strong system\nover the eastern U.S. and a developing shortwave off the Pacific \nnorthwest. Ultimately, the timing of this system, and our precip \nchances over the weekend, will depend on what that low system to \nthe east does, as well as how the shortwave to the northwest \ndevelops. Should the eastern system slow or stall over the Ohio \nValley area, the ridge hanging over us would have nowhere to go, \nkeeping things drier and warmer over the weekend. Similarly, if \nthe shortwave dissipates before reaching us, as the GFS solution \nwould suggest, our precip chances would be low. On the other hand,\nshould that shortwave to the northwest strengthen and persist, as\nthe ECMWF would suggest, we might be able to get some precip \nchances and cooler temperatures into the area.",
